    <string name="stats_mean_help">Your average level per observed day. It tracks the overall trend, but a few very high days can pull it up.</string>
    <string name="stats_median_help">Your most representative day: half of your days sit below it and half above. Useful when a few high episodes distort the mean.</string>
    <string name="stats_q1_help">25 % of observed days are at this level or below. A realistic reference point for your lighter days.</string>
    <string name="stats_q3_help">75 % of observed days are at this level or below. Above it, you are in the highest quarter of your days.</string>
    <string name="stats_p90_help">90 % of observed days are at this level or below. Higher values are your top 10 % of days and can help spot exceptional episodes.</string>
    <string name="stats_stddev_help">Shows how much your days vary around the mean. A low value means a steady rhythm; a high value means large swings from one day to the next.</string>
    <string name="stats_cv_help">Relates the variability to your mean. Handy for seeing whether your rhythm is getting steadier even as your average level changes.</string>
    <string name="stats_minimum_help">Your lowest actually observed day. Explicitly logged sober days can therefore give a value of zero.</string>
    <string name="stats_maximum_help">Your highest observed day. It shows the scale of your historical peak, without making it a record to beat.</string>

